<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reuss (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reuss is a river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, about 160 kilometres long. Rising near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard Pass</a>, it flows through the canton of Uri and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen</a> gorge into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>, leaves the lake at the city of Lucerne and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a>. Its valley carried the Gotthard route that made Uri and Lucerne important from the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reuss-river.jpg" alt="Reuss (river)" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edwin Lee · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kapellbrucke_in_Lucerne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Reuss is one of the four major rivers that rise in the Gotthard region, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, and its drainage basin of 3,426 square kilometres roughly covers Central Switzerland. The Gotthardreuss emerges from Lago di Lucendro, a reservoir built in 1947, and the Furkareuss rises east of the Furka Pass, only about 5 kilometres from the source of the Rhône, though it flows to the other side of the continent. They join at Hospental, and below Andermatt the river turns north through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen Gorge</a>, which is 3 kilometres long and narrow and deep, beneath the Devil's Bridge.</p><p>From Göschenen it forms the main valley of the canton of Uri and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>. It leaves the lake at Lucerne, where the Kapellbrücke, first built in 1333, and the Spreuerbrücke of 1408 cross it. It joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> just downstream of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a>, and that river flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ptolemy records the river's pre-Germanic name as Silana, from which the name of Silenen derives. The Germanic name appears as Rusa or Rusia from the 9th century. Until the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen Gorge</a> was impassable and separated Urseren from Uri. It was made passable in the 1230s, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard Pass</a>, and the Reuss valley gained immense strategic value, which is reflected in the grant of imperial immediacy to Uri and in the founding of the Old Swiss Confederacy.</p><p>The lower valley lay under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in the 14th century. In the 15th century the river became a border between territories of the Swiss Confederacy, first in 1429 between Lucerne and Zürich, and downstream of Ottenbach it came to lie within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> when that canton was created in 1803. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the Reuss was an imperial road and served as a waterway for traffic between Lucerne and Zurzach and, by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, Basel, until transit roads were built in the 18th century. In 1528 the Catholic cantons blocked the trade routes across the river for the Protestant cantons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Because ferries and bridges brought in income, new crossings were opposed in the early modern period, and only one new bridge was built, at Sins in 1640. More than a dozen crossings exist now, including railway and motorway bridges. The river was canalised between Attinghausen and Altdorf in 1850–1863 and to its mouth in 1900–1912, which greatly increased arable land. A large river-engineering project was completed during 1971–1985. Hydroelectric plants have been built at Amsteg (1922), Wassen (1949) and Göschenen (1962).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reuss (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lucerne grew around the monastery of Saint Leodegar where the <b>Reuss</b> leaves Lake Lucerne, and boomed after the opening of the Gotthard route in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Canton of Lucerne</a></li><li><blockquote>In 853 Louis the German gave the <b>Reuss</b> valley of Uri to the Fraumünster abbey of Zürich.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a></li></ul></section>
